<!-- wp:heading {"level":2} -->
<h2 class="wp-block-heading"><strong>A DPS Primer</strong></h2>
<!-- /wp:heading -->

<!-- wp:quote -->
<blockquote class="wp-block-quote"><!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p><strong>CLI baseline:</strong> The examples use Azure IoT CLI preview extension
<code>azure-iot</code> <strong>1.0.0b2</strong>, released <strong>6 October 2026</strong>. Last
reviewed against current Microsoft Learn guidance <strong>5 November 2026</strong>.
<a href="https://github.com/Azure/azure-iot-cli-extension/releases/tag/v1.0.0b2">Release notes</a>.</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph --></blockquote>
<!-- /wp:quote -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>Welcome back to this series of posts exploring the latest features of Azure IoT Hub, Device Provisioning Service (DPS), and Azure Device Registry (ADR).</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>The previous post introduced the new features and gave an overview of what we can expect in this post series.</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>In this post, we'll dive deeper in the Azure Device Provisioning Service and how we can use the new features.</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:heading {"level":3} -->
<h3 class="wp-block-heading">What's new in this edition?</h3>
<!-- /wp:heading -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>One thing that's easy to mix up: the device first proves its identity to DPS;
if you enable certificate management, ADR can then issue a separate
certificate for use with IoT Hub. You don't need certificate management for
the IoT Hub preview, but you do need DPS. I've updated the primer to make that
distinction clearer. See the <a href="https://www.petecodes.co.uk/iot-hub-with-adr-and-certificate-management-part-1">Introduction</a>
for the series-wide changes.</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>The device code now uses <code>Microsoft.Azure.Iot.Device</code> <strong>2.0.0-preview4</strong>:
one unified <code>ConnectionClient</code> for DPS and the Hub, and <code>TelemetryClient</code> for
our messages. I've removed the custom C# provisioning protocol, not the
configuration and durable CSR/key/chain handling. Standard symmetric-key/X.509
and both CSR flows remain on the existing Hub, with its endpoint/profile discovered
through DPS. This is still a preview;
the <a href="https://github.com/Azure/azure-iot-sdk/blob/main/dotnet/README.md">SDK matrix</a>
also makes clear that DPS software updates are a separate, unsupported SDK
workflow. Our separate updater isn't replaced by this change.</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:separator -->
<hr class="wp-block-separator has-alpha-channel-opacity"/>
<!-- /wp:separator -->

<!-- wp:quote -->
<blockquote class="wp-block-quote"><!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p><strong>Note:</strong> This guide uses <strong>PowerShell</strong> for all commands and scripts. While Azure CLI commands work identically in bash/zsh, PowerShell is recommended for Windows environments and provides consistent cross-platform support. If you're using Linux/macOS, you can install <a href="https://learn.microsoft.com/powershell/scripting/install/installing-powershell">PowerShell Core</a> or adapt the commands to bash syntax.</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph --></blockquote>
<!-- /wp:quote -->

<!-- wp:separator -->
<hr class="wp-block-separator has-alpha-channel-opacity"/>
<!-- /wp:separator -->

<!-- wp:heading {"level":3} -->
<h3 class="wp-block-heading">Contents</h3>
<!-- /wp:heading -->

<!-- wp:list {"ordered":true} -->
<ol class="wp-block-list"><!-- wp:list-item -->
<li><a href="https://www.petecodes.co.uk/iot-hub-with-adr-and-certificate-management-part-1">Introduction</a></li>
<!-- /wp:list-item -->
<!-- wp:list-item -->
<li><a href="https://www.petecodes.co.uk/iot-hub-with-adr-and-certificate-management-part-2">A DPS Primer (this post)</a></li>
<!-- /wp:list-item -->
<!-- wp:list-item -->
<li><a href="https://www.petecodes.co.uk/iot-hub-with-adr-and-certificate-management-part-3">Creating DPS, IoT Hub and ADR Instances</a></li>
<!-- /wp:list-item -->
<!-- wp:list-item -->
<li><a href="https://www.petecodes.co.uk/iot-hub-with-adr-and-certificate-management-part-4">Understanding X.509 and CSR Workflows</a></li>
<!-- /wp:list-item -->
<!-- wp:list-item -->
<li><a href="https://www.petecodes.co.uk/iot-hub-with-adr-and-certificate-management-part-5">The Device Application</a></li>
<!-- /wp:list-item -->
</ol>
<!-- /wp:list -->

<!-- wp:heading {"level":3} -->
<h3 class="wp-block-heading">What is Device Provisioning Service?</h3>
<!-- /wp:heading -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>Let's start with the basics. Before we write code or create Azure resources, you need to understand what DPS does and why it matters for your IoT deployment.</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>Azure IoT Hub Device Provisioning Service (DPS) is a helper service for IoT Hub that enables zero-touch, just-in-time provisioning to the right IoT hub without requiring human intervention.</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:heading {"level":3} -->
<h3 class="wp-block-heading">Why Use DPS?</h3>
<!-- /wp:heading -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>You might be wondering: &quot;Can't I just register devices manually in IoT Hub?&quot; You can, but let's see why that doesn't scale:</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:heading {"level":4} -->
<h4 class="wp-block-heading">Traditional Approach (Manual Registration)</h4>
<!-- /wp:heading -->

<!-- wp:list -->
<ul class="wp-block-list"><!-- wp:list-item -->
<li>Register each device manually in IoT Hub</li>
<!-- /wp:list-item -->
<!-- wp:list-item -->
<li>Hardcode connection strings in device firmware</li>
<!-- /wp:list-item -->
<!-- wp:list-item -->
<li>Difficult to reassign devices to different hubs</li>
<!-- /wp:list-item -->
<!-- wp:list-item -->
<li>Doesn't scale beyond a few devices</li>
<!-- /wp:list-item -->
<!-- wp:list-item -->
<li>Manual certificate management and rotation</li>
<!-- /wp:list-item -->
</ul>
<!-- /wp:list -->

<!-- wp:heading {"level":4} -->
<h4 class="wp-block-heading">DPS Approach (Zero-Touch Provisioning)</h4>
<!-- /wp:heading -->

<!-- wp:list -->
<ul class="wp-block-list"><!-- wp:list-item -->
<li>Devices automatically register themselves</li>
<!-- /wp:list-item -->
<!-- wp:list-item -->
<li>No connection strings hardcoded</li>
<!-- /wp:list-item -->
<!-- wp:list-item -->
<li>Devices can be reassigned dynamically</li>
<!-- /wp:list-item -->
<!-- wp:list-item -->
<li>Scales to millions of devices</li>
<!-- /wp:list-item -->
<!-- wp:list-item -->
<li>Automated certificate issuance and lifecycle management</li>
<!-- /wp:list-item -->
</ul>
<!-- /wp:list -->

<!-- wp:heading {"level":3} -->
<h3 class="wp-block-heading">Key Benefits</h3>
<!-- /wp:heading -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>Now that you understand the difference, here are the concrete advantages of using DPS:</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:list -->
<ul class="wp-block-list"><!-- wp:list-item -->
<li><strong>Scale:</strong> Provision thousands or millions of devices without manual intervention. Devices can self-register when they first come online.</li>
<!-- /wp:list-item -->
<!-- wp:list-item -->
<li><strong>Security:</strong> No connection strings hardcoded in device firmware. Devices use cryptographic attestation (symmetric keys, TPM, or X.509 certificates) to prove identity.</li>
<!-- /wp:list-item -->
<!-- wp:list-item -->
<li><strong>Flexibility:</strong> Devices can be reassigned to different IoT Hubs based on business logic, geolocation, or load balancing.</li>
<!-- /wp:list-item -->
<!-- wp:list-item -->
<li><strong>Multi-tenancy:</strong> Different devices can automatically be directed to different IoT Hubs based on enrollment configuration.</li>
<!-- /wp:list-item -->
<!-- wp:list-item -->
<li><strong>Separation:</strong> Separation between bootstrap and operational credentials for devices.</li>
<!-- /wp:list-item -->
</ul>
<!-- /wp:list -->

<!-- wp:heading {"level":4} -->
<h4 class="wp-block-heading">Optional ADR Certificate Management (Preview)</h4>
<!-- /wp:heading -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>When certificate management is enabled, DPS can coordinate ADR issuance of operational X.509 certificates during provisioning using a Certificate Signing Request (CSR). This optional capability doesn't replace the device's onboarding credential. You can use a Microsoft-managed root CA or bring your own CA; DPS remains required for every IoT Hub preview scenario, whether certificate management is enabled or not. See the <a href="https://learn.microsoft.com/en-gb/azure/iot/iot-certificate-management-overview">certificate management overview</a>.</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:heading {"level":3} -->
<h3 class="wp-block-heading">What's New: Microsoft Certificate Management</h3>
<!-- /wp:heading -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>The unified SDK now handles this repository's CSR provisioning flow. The
earlier custom transport used the <code>2025-07-01-preview</code> API
for its CSR provisioning example. The current service workflow is that DPS
validates the device's onboarding credential and coordinates issuance of an
<strong>operational certificate</strong> from the ADR namespace's issuing CA:</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:heading {"level":4} -->
<h4 class="wp-block-heading">Traditional X.509 Workflow</h4>
<!-- /wp:heading -->

<!-- wp:canaryblue/mermaid-diagram {"diagram":"graph TD\n    CA[\u0022Your CA (PKI)\u0022] \u002d\u002d\u003e|creates| Chain[\u0022Root and intermediate certificates\u0022]\n    CA \u002d\u002d\u003e|issues ahead of time| DeviceCert[\u0022Per-device X.509 certificate\u0022]\n    Chain \u002d\u002d\u003e|trusted by| DPS[\u0022DPS enrollment\u0022]\n    DeviceCert \u002d\u002d\u003e|installed on| Device[\u0022Device\u0022]\n    Device \u002d\u002d\u003e|onboards with X.509| DPS\n    DPS \u002d\u002d\u003e|assigns| Hub[\u0022IoT Hub\u0022]\n    Device \u002d\u002d\u003e|connects with X.509| Hub"} -->
<div class="wp-block-canaryblue-mermaid-diagram is-theme-default" data-mermaid-label="Mermaid diagram" data-mermaid-theme="default"><pre class="wp-block-canaryblue-mermaid-diagram__source" data-mermaid-source="true">graph TD
    CA[&quot;Your CA (PKI)&quot;] --&gt;|creates| Chain[&quot;Root and intermediate certificates&quot;]
    CA --&gt;|issues ahead of time| DeviceCert[&quot;Per-device X.509 certificate&quot;]
    Chain --&gt;|trusted by| DPS[&quot;DPS enrollment&quot;]
    DeviceCert --&gt;|installed on| Device[&quot;Device&quot;]
    Device --&gt;|onboards with X.509| DPS
    DPS --&gt;|assigns| Hub[&quot;IoT Hub&quot;]
    Device --&gt;|connects with X.509| Hub</pre><div class="wp-block-canaryblue-mermaid-diagram__output"></div></div>
<!-- /wp:canaryblue/mermaid-diagram -->

<!-- wp:heading {"level":4} -->
<h4 class="wp-block-heading">🆕 New CSR-Based Workflow</h4>
<!-- /wp:heading -->

<!-- wp:canaryblue/mermaid-diagram {"diagram":"flowchart TB\n    subgraph enrollment[\u0022Enrollment setup\u0022]\n        direction LR\n        DPSProfile[\u0022DPS enrollment\u0022] \u002d\u002d\u003e|references policy| Policy[\u0022ADR certificate policy\u0022]\n    end\n\n    subgraph request[\u0022Device onboarding\u0022]\n        direction LR\n        DeviceRequest[\u0022Device: generate keypair and CSR\u0022] \u002d\u002d\u003e|CSR and onboarding attestation| DPSValidate[\u0022DPS: validate credential\u0022]\n    end\n\n    subgraph issuance[\u0022Operational certificate issuance\u0022]\n        direction LR\n        ADR[\u0022ADR: select policy's issuing ICA\u0022] \u002d\u002d\u003e|use policy| CA[\u0022Microsoft-managed CA: issue chain\u0022]\n        CA \u002d\u002d\u003e|issued chain| ADRReturn[\u0022ADR: return chain to DPS\u0022]\n    end\n\n    subgraph return[\u0022Return to device\u0022]\n        direction LR\n        DPSReturn[\u0022DPS: return chain and Hub assignment\u0022] \u002d\u002d\u003e DeviceInstall[\u0022Device: install chain\u0022]\n    end\n\n    Hub[\u0022IoT Hub\u0022]\n    DPSProfile \u002d\u002d\u003e DPSValidate\n    Policy \u002d\u002d\u003e ADR\n    DPSValidate \u002d\u002d\u003e|request issuance| ADR\n    ADRReturn \u002d\u002d\u003e|return chain| DPSReturn\n    DeviceInstall \u002d\u002d\u003e|connect with operational X.509| Hub\n    ADR \u002d\u002d\u003e|synchronize CA certificates| Hub"} -->
<div class="wp-block-canaryblue-mermaid-diagram is-theme-default" data-mermaid-label="Mermaid diagram" data-mermaid-theme="default"><pre class="wp-block-canaryblue-mermaid-diagram__source" data-mermaid-source="true">flowchart TB
    subgraph enrollment[&quot;Enrollment setup&quot;]
        direction LR
        DPSProfile[&quot;DPS enrollment&quot;] --&gt;|references policy| Policy[&quot;ADR certificate policy&quot;]
    end

    subgraph request[&quot;Device onboarding&quot;]
        direction LR
        DeviceRequest[&quot;Device: generate keypair and CSR&quot;] --&gt;|CSR and onboarding attestation| DPSValidate[&quot;DPS: validate credential&quot;]
    end

    subgraph issuance[&quot;Operational certificate issuance&quot;]
        direction LR
        ADR[&quot;ADR: select policy's issuing ICA&quot;] --&gt;|use policy| CA[&quot;Microsoft-managed CA: issue chain&quot;]
        CA --&gt;|issued chain| ADRReturn[&quot;ADR: return chain to DPS&quot;]
    end

    subgraph return[&quot;Return to device&quot;]
        direction LR
        DPSReturn[&quot;DPS: return chain and Hub assignment&quot;] --&gt; DeviceInstall[&quot;Device: install chain&quot;]
    end

    Hub[&quot;IoT Hub&quot;]
    DPSProfile --&gt; DPSValidate
    Policy --&gt; ADR
    DPSValidate --&gt;|request issuance| ADR
    ADRReturn --&gt;|return chain| DPSReturn
    DeviceInstall --&gt;|connect with operational X.509| Hub
    ADR --&gt;|synchronize CA certificates| Hub</pre><div class="wp-block-canaryblue-mermaid-diagram__output"></div></div>
<!-- /wp:canaryblue/mermaid-diagram -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>The device generates the CSR and keeps its private key; it never leaves the
device. Its onboarding credential authenticates to DPS, while the enrollment
references the separate ADR certificate policy for operational issuance. The
policy belongs to an ADR intermediate CA and controls device-certificate
validity. Current IoT Hub workflows support operational-certificate renewal
without reprovisioning; follow the current service documentation for the device
flow.</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p><strong>Key advantages:</strong></p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:list -->
<ul class="wp-block-list"><!-- wp:list-item -->
<li>No need to manage your own CA infrastructure</li>
<!-- /wp:list-item -->
<!-- wp:list-item -->
<li>Private keys never leave the device</li>
<!-- /wp:list-item -->
<!-- wp:list-item -->
<li>Certificates issued just-in-time</li>
<!-- /wp:list-item -->
<!-- wp:list-item -->
<li>Automated lifecycle management</li>
<!-- /wp:list-item -->
<!-- wp:list-item -->
<li>Reduced operational complexity</li>
<!-- /wp:list-item -->
</ul>
<!-- /wp:list -->

<!-- wp:heading {"level":3} -->
<h3 class="wp-block-heading">Azure Device Registry (ADR) Integration</h3>
<!-- /wp:heading -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>Azure Device Registry (ADR) is Microsoft's <strong>centralized device registry and
management plane</strong>. It represents IoT Hub devices as Azure Resource Manager
resources, enabling governance through RBAC, tags, resource groups, and
cross-hub queries with Azure Resource Graph.</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>ADR is generally available for Azure IoT Operations. Its integration with
Azure IoT Hub is a separate <strong>public preview</strong>. Each IoT Hub links to one ADR
namespace, while multiple hubs can share a namespace. You can create a new Hub
or connect an eligible existing Hub. Connecting an existing Hub to a namespace
is permanent and can't be reversed. This series' setup script creates a
dedicated new Hub; see the <a href="https://learn.microsoft.com/en-gb/azure/iot/device-registry/get-started-azure-device-registry">current getting-started
guide</a>
for the existing-resource path.</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:heading {"level":4} -->
<h4 class="wp-block-heading">What is ADR?</h4>
<!-- /wp:heading -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>ADR is a <strong>device identity and management layer</strong> that:</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:list -->
<ul class="wp-block-list"><!-- wp:list-item -->
<li>Stores device metadata, attributes, and tags</li>
<!-- /wp:list-item -->
<!-- wp:list-item -->
<li>Manages credential policies for certificate issuance</li>
<!-- /wp:list-item -->
<!-- wp:list-item -->
<li>Decouples device identity from IoT Hub</li>
<!-- /wp:list-item -->
<!-- wp:list-item -->
<li>Enables multi-hub scenarios</li>
<!-- /wp:list-item -->
<!-- wp:list-item -->
<li>Provides query and update APIs</li>
<!-- /wp:list-item -->
</ul>
<!-- /wp:list -->

<!-- wp:heading {"level":3} -->
<h3 class="wp-block-heading">What is an ADR Namespace?</h3>
<!-- /wp:heading -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>An ADR namespace establishes a management and security boundary for devices represented in ADR.</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>A namespace can include devices connecting to different IoT Hub instances, and is where you can enable new features like Microsoft-backed X.509 certificate management (preview).</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:heading {"level":4} -->
<h4 class="wp-block-heading">Why ADR Matters</h4>
<!-- /wp:heading -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>In traditional IoT Hub deployments:</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:list -->
<ul class="wp-block-list"><!-- wp:list-item -->
<li>Device identity is tightly coupled to a specific IoT Hub</li>
<!-- /wp:list-item -->
<!-- wp:list-item -->
<li>Moving devices between hubs is complex</li>
<!-- /wp:list-item -->
<!-- wp:list-item -->
<li>Credential management is manual</li>
<!-- /wp:list-item -->
<!-- wp:list-item -->
<li>Metadata is limited</li>
<!-- /wp:list-item -->
</ul>
<!-- /wp:list -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>With ADR:</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:list -->
<ul class="wp-block-list"><!-- wp:list-item -->
<li>✅ Devices are represented and governed as Azure resources</li>
<!-- /wp:list-item -->
<!-- wp:list-item -->
<li>✅ Namespaces provide a shared registry and security boundary</li>
<!-- /wp:list-item -->
<!-- wp:list-item -->
<li>✅ Credential policies support certificate issuance</li>
<!-- /wp:list-item -->
<!-- wp:list-item -->
<li>✅ Query and manage devices across hubs sharing a namespace</li>
<!-- /wp:list-item -->
</ul>
<!-- /wp:list -->

<!-- wp:heading {"level":3} -->
<h3 class="wp-block-heading">Certificate Management: Onboarding and Operational Credentials</h3>
<!-- /wp:heading -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>Certificate management handles <strong>operational X.509 certificates</strong> used by a
device to authenticate to IoT Hub after provisioning. It does not issue or
manage the device's onboarding credential. A device first authenticates to DPS
with an onboarding method such as a symmetric key, TPM, or an existing X.509
certificate, then submits a CSR as part of the provisioning transaction.
DPS validates the onboarding credential and uses the ADR CA and certificate
policy referenced by the enrollment; the policy's intermediate CA signs the
operational certificate. The device private key remains on the device. Each
ADR intermediate CA can have one certificate policy.</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>Certificate management is optional, but <strong>DPS is required for all IoT Hub
preview scenarios</strong>, even when certificate management isn't enabled.
Operational certificates can be renewed and revoked, and ADR manages CA
lifecycle and rotation operations. IoT Hub integration remains in public
preview and isn't recommended for production workloads.</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:heading {"level":3} -->
<h3 class="wp-block-heading">Attestation Methods</h3>
<!-- /wp:heading -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>Attestation is how devices prove their identity to DPS. Think of it like showing ID before boarding a flight. DPS supports three different &quot;ID types&quot;:</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:heading {"level":4} -->
<h4 class="wp-block-heading">1. Symmetric Key</h4>
<!-- /wp:heading -->

<!-- wp:list -->
<ul class="wp-block-list"><!-- wp:list-item -->
<li><strong>Simplest method</strong></li>
<!-- /wp:list-item -->
<!-- wp:list-item -->
<li>Uses shared secret (enrollment group key)</li>
<!-- /wp:list-item -->
<!-- wp:list-item -->
<li>Device derives individual key: <code>HMACSHA256(groupKey, registrationId)</code></li>
<!-- /wp:list-item -->
<!-- wp:list-item -->
<li>Good for development and testing</li>
<!-- /wp:list-item -->
<!-- wp:list-item -->
<li>Less secure than certificate-based methods</li>
<!-- /wp:list-item -->
<!-- wp:list-item -->
<li>🆕 <strong>Can be combined with CSR</strong> - authenticate with symmetric key, receive X.509 certificate</li>
<!-- /wp:list-item -->
</ul>
<!-- /wp:list -->

<!-- wp:heading {"level":4} -->
<h4 class="wp-block-heading">2. TPM (Trusted Platform Module)</h4>
<!-- /wp:heading -->

<!-- wp:list -->
<ul class="wp-block-list"><!-- wp:list-item -->
<li>Uses hardware security module</li>
<!-- /wp:list-item -->
<!-- wp:list-item -->
<li>Very secure</li>
<!-- /wp:list-item -->
<!-- wp:list-item -->
<li>Requires TPM chip on device</li>
<!-- /wp:list-item -->
<!-- wp:list-item -->
<li>Unique per device</li>
<!-- /wp:list-item -->
<!-- wp:list-item -->
<li>Not covered in this series</li>
<!-- /wp:list-item -->
</ul>
<!-- /wp:list -->

<!-- wp:heading {"level":4} -->
<h4 class="wp-block-heading">3. X.509 Certificate</h4>
<!-- /wp:heading -->

<!-- wp:list -->
<ul class="wp-block-list"><!-- wp:list-item -->
<li><strong>Industry standard for security</strong></li>
<!-- /wp:list-item -->
<!-- wp:list-item -->
<li>Uses public key infrastructure (PKI)</li>
<!-- /wp:list-item -->
<!-- wp:list-item -->
<li>Supports certificate rotation</li>
<!-- /wp:list-item -->
<!-- wp:list-item -->
<li>Scalable with enrollment groups</li>
<!-- /wp:list-item -->
<!-- wp:list-item -->
<li><strong>Two approaches:</strong><!-- wp:list -->
<ul class="wp-block-list"><!-- wp:list-item -->
<li><strong>Traditional:</strong> Pre-provisioned certificates</li>
<!-- /wp:list-item -->
<!-- wp:list-item -->
<li><strong>🆕 CSR-based:</strong> Certificates issued during provisioning</li>
<!-- /wp:list-item -->
</ul>
<!-- /wp:list -->
</li>
<!-- /wp:list-item -->
<!-- wp:list-item -->
<li><strong>This is what we'll focus on</strong></li>
<!-- /wp:list-item -->
</ul>
<!-- /wp:list -->

<!-- wp:heading {"level":3} -->
<h3 class="wp-block-heading">High-Level Provisioning Flow with ADR</h3>
<!-- /wp:heading -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>Here's how the complete flow works with the new features:</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>The flow is shown in two stages to keep each diagram readable at article width.</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p><strong>Certificate issuance</strong></p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:canaryblue/mermaid-diagram {"diagram":"sequenceDiagram\n    participant Device\n    participant DPS\n    participant ADR\n    participant CA as Issuing CA\n    Device->>Device: Create keypair and CSR\n    Device->>DPS: Attest and submit CSR\n    DPS->>DPS: Validate enrollment\n    DPS->>ADR: Request issuance by policy\n    ADR->>CA: Select policy's issuing ICA\n    CA\u002d\u002d\u003e>ADR: Issue certificate chain\n    ADR\u002d\u002d\u003e>DPS: Return certificate chain\n    DPS\u002d\u002d\u003e>Device: Return chain and Hub assignment"} -->
<div class="wp-block-canaryblue-mermaid-diagram is-theme-default" data-mermaid-label="Mermaid diagram" data-mermaid-theme="default"><pre class="wp-block-canaryblue-mermaid-diagram__source" data-mermaid-source="true">sequenceDiagram
    participant Device
    participant DPS
    participant ADR
    participant CA as Issuing CA
    Device-&gt;&gt;Device: Create keypair and CSR
    Device-&gt;&gt;DPS: Attest and submit CSR
    DPS-&gt;&gt;DPS: Validate enrollment
    DPS-&gt;&gt;ADR: Request issuance by policy
    ADR-&gt;&gt;CA: Select policy's issuing ICA
    CA--&gt;&gt;ADR: Issue certificate chain
    ADR--&gt;&gt;DPS: Return certificate chain
    DPS--&gt;&gt;Device: Return chain and Hub assignment</pre><div class="wp-block-canaryblue-mermaid-diagram__output"></div></div>
<!-- /wp:canaryblue/mermaid-diagram -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p><strong>IoT Hub connection</strong></p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:canaryblue/mermaid-diagram {"diagram":"sequenceDiagram\n    participant Device\n    participant ADR\n    participant Hub as IoT Hub\n    Device->>Device: Install chain and retain private key\n    ADR->>Hub: Synchronize CA certificates\n    Device->>Hub: Connect with operational X.509\n    Hub\u002d\u002d\u003e>Device: Authenticate device"} -->
<div class="wp-block-canaryblue-mermaid-diagram is-theme-default" data-mermaid-label="Mermaid diagram" data-mermaid-theme="default"><pre class="wp-block-canaryblue-mermaid-diagram__source" data-mermaid-source="true">sequenceDiagram
    participant Device
    participant ADR
    participant Hub as IoT Hub
    Device-&gt;&gt;Device: Install chain and retain private key
    ADR-&gt;&gt;Hub: Synchronize CA certificates
    Device-&gt;&gt;Hub: Connect with operational X.509
    Hub--&gt;&gt;Device: Authenticate device</pre><div class="wp-block-canaryblue-mermaid-diagram__output"></div></div>
<!-- /wp:canaryblue/mermaid-diagram -->

<!-- wp:heading {"level":3} -->
<h3 class="wp-block-heading">Alternative: Symmetric Key + X.509</h3>
<!-- /wp:heading -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>This series defaults to X.509 bootstrap attestation followed by CSR-based X.509
operational certificate issuance. Symmetric-key onboarding is a supported
alternative, not the default for this walkthrough.</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>One powerful pattern enabled by the new API:</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:list {"ordered":true} -->
<ol class="wp-block-list"><!-- wp:list-item -->
<li><strong>Phase 1 - Provisioning:</strong> Device authenticates to DPS using <strong>symmetric key</strong> (simple, pre-shared)</li>
<!-- /wp:list-item -->
<!-- wp:list-item -->
<li><strong>Phase 2 - Operation:</strong> Device connects to IoT Hub using <strong>X.509 certificate</strong> (secure, Microsoft-issued)</li>
<!-- /wp:list-item -->
</ol>
<!-- /wp:list -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p><strong>Benefits:</strong></p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:list -->
<ul class="wp-block-list"><!-- wp:list-item -->
<li>Easy initial provisioning (no pre-generated certificates)</li>
<!-- /wp:list-item -->
<!-- wp:list-item -->
<li>Secure operational communication (X.509)</li>
<!-- /wp:list-item -->
<!-- wp:list-item -->
<li>No need to distribute device certificates ahead of time</li>
<!-- /wp:list-item -->
<!-- wp:list-item -->
<li>Best of both worlds</li>
<!-- /wp:list-item -->
</ul>
<!-- /wp:list -->

<!-- wp:heading {"level":3} -->
<h3 class="wp-block-heading">When to Use DPS</h3>
<!-- /wp:heading -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>✅ <strong>Use DPS when:</strong></p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:list -->
<ul class="wp-block-list"><!-- wp:list-item -->
<li>Deploying many devices (10+)</li>
<!-- /wp:list-item -->
<!-- wp:list-item -->
<li>Devices need to be reassigned between hubs</li>
<!-- /wp:list-item -->
<!-- wp:list-item -->
<li>You want zero-touch provisioning</li>
<!-- /wp:list-item -->
<!-- wp:list-item -->
<li>Security is important (no hardcoded secrets)</li>
<!-- /wp:list-item -->
<!-- wp:list-item -->
<li>You need multi-tenancy support</li>
<!-- /wp:list-item -->
<!-- wp:list-item -->
<li>You want automated certificate management</li>
<!-- /wp:list-item -->
<!-- wp:list-item -->
<li>🆕 You want Microsoft-managed certificate issuance</li>
<!-- /wp:list-item -->
</ul>
<!-- /wp:list -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>🤔 <strong>Consider if DPS is right for you if:</strong></p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:list -->
<ul class="wp-block-list"><!-- wp:list-item -->
<li>Only a few devices (&lt; 10)</li>
<!-- /wp:list-item -->
<!-- wp:list-item -->
<li>Devices never move between hubs</li>
<!-- /wp:list-item -->
<!-- wp:list-item -->
<li>Prototyping with connection strings is acceptable</li>
<!-- /wp:list-item -->
</ul>
<!-- /wp:list -->

<!-- wp:heading {"level":3} -->
<h3 class="wp-block-heading">Preview API Status</h3>
<!-- /wp:heading -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>The sample application in this series uses the <strong><code>2025-07-01-preview</code> API</strong>:</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:list -->
<ul class="wp-block-list"><!-- wp:list-item -->
<li>✅ CSR-based certificate issuance</li>
<!-- /wp:list-item -->
<!-- wp:list-item -->
<li>✅ ADR credential policies</li>
<!-- /wp:list-item -->
<!-- wp:list-item -->
<li>✅ DPS MQTT protocol support</li>
<!-- /wp:list-item -->
<!-- wp:list-item -->
<li>✅ Microsoft-managed CA</li>
<!-- /wp:list-item -->
</ul>
<!-- /wp:list -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>IoT Hub integration with ADR and certificate management is still in <strong>public
preview</strong> and isn't recommended for production workloads. The sorter now uses
the official unified SDK directly, with helpers for durable credentials. Don't treat old
wire-level examples as instructions to rebuild the removed transport.</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:quote -->
<blockquote class="wp-block-quote"><!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p><strong>Note:</strong> Check <a href="https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/iot-hub/iot-hub-what-is-new">Microsoft's current IoT Hub preview guidance</a> before deployment.</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph --></blockquote>
<!-- /wp:quote -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>For the current setup, see Microsoft's <a href="https://learn.microsoft.com/en-gb/azure/iot/device-registry/get-started-azure-device-registry">ADR connected-services guide</a>, <a href="https://learn.microsoft.com/en-gb/azure/iot/concept-managed-ca-hierarchy">CA hierarchy overview</a>, <a href="https://learn.microsoft.com/en-gb/azure/iot/how-to-create-policy?tabs=cli">managed CA setup</a>, and <a href="https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/iot/how-to-create-edit-certificate-policy?tabs=cli">certificate-policy and DPS-enrollment guide</a>.</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

